.TH PILA 1 "" "Pila" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pila \- a stacking Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pila
.RB [ \-c | \-\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.BR winit | udev | headless ]
.RB [ \-\-headless\-outputs
.IR OUTPUTS ]
.br
.B pila \-\-check\-config
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pila msg
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I request
.br
.B pila\-session
.br
.B pila\-report
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pila
is a stacking Wayland compositor: overlapping windows with title bars, snapping, workspaces
that span all monitors, animations and per-app rules, configured in one TOML file that applies
when saved.
X11 apps run through Xwayland, which Pila starts when the first one connects (and, with
.B [xwayland] idle-timeout
set, stops again once none is left);
.B [xwayland] enabled = false
turns them off.
The default
.B autostart
runs swayidle, which locks the screen with swaylock after 5 minutes without input and before
the computer sleeps, and turns the monitors off after 10; any input turns them back on.
.PP
Started from a login screen or a TTY, Pila drives the monitors and input devices itself.
Started inside another desktop, it opens as a window there (nested), with Alt as its Mod key.
.PP
.B pila\-session
starts Pila as a login session: with its log in
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/pila.log ,
and a D-Bus session bus of its own (with dbus-run-session) when the system gives none.
Login screens run it; from a TTY, run it after logging in.
.PP
.B pila\-report
gathers what a bug report needs (version, system, GPU, monitors, whether the configuration is
valid, the end of the log) into
.I file
(default:
.IR pila\-report.txt ),
without the user name, host name and home directory.
It sends nothing anywhere.
.PP
The user manual,
.IR MANUAL.md ,
describes how to use and set up Pila; Pila's packages install it with the documentation
(usually in
.IR /usr/share/doc/pila ),
and it's on https://github.com/igalmarino/pila.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Run the shell command
.I CMD
at startup, instead of the configuration's
.B autostart
(which defaults to waybar, mako and swayidle).
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the default (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " " \fIBACKEND\fR
.B winit
opens Pila as a window in another Wayland or X11 session;
.B udev
drives the monitors and input devices directly, from a TTY;
.B headless
has virtual outputs and a control socket
(\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fP/\fIWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fP.control),
for automated tests.
The default is
.B winit
if
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
or
.B DISPLAY
is set, else
.BR udev .
.TP
.BR \-\-headless\-outputs " " \fIOUTPUTS\fR
The headless backend's outputs:
.IR NAME : W x H [@ HZ ][= MAKE / MODEL [/ SERIAL ]],
separated by commas (default: HEADLESS\-1:1280x800@60).
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Check the configuration file as Pila would read it, the keyboard layout included, print what's
wrong, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.SH PILA MSG
.B pila msg
talks to a running Pila:
.TP
.BR "pila msg " [ \-\-json ] " windows" | outputs | workspaces | keyboard | colors | switch | focused | version
List the windows, monitors, workspaces, keyboard layouts, the colors of
.B [colors]
or the windows of a Mod+Tab in progress, or show the focused window or the version.
.TP
.BR "pila msg " [ \-\-json ] " subscribe " [ windows | workspaces | outputs | keyboard | colors | switch ...]
Print events as they happen: the whole list of windows, workspaces, monitors, keyboard layouts,
colors or the window switch each time it changes, until Pila quits.
.TP
.BI "pila msg action " action
Run an action, as in a key binding (for example
.BR "workspace 2" ).
.TP
.BI "pila msg focus " id ", pila msg close " id ", pila msg minimize " id
Focus a window, ask it to close or minimize it, by the
.I id
.B pila msg windows
gives (focusing a minimized window brings it back).
.PP
Scripts should use
.BR \-\-json :
the plain text is for people and may change.
.B pila msg \-\-help
has the details.
Exit status: 0 on success, 1 if Pila refused the request, 2 for wrong arguments, 3 if it can't
reach Pila.
.SH DEFAULT BINDINGS
Mod is Super from a login screen or a TTY and Alt nested, unless the configuration's
.B mod
says otherwise. With
.B default-bindings = false
in the configuration there are none of these (nor the default gestures), only the
configuration's own.
.TP
.B Mod+Return
Open a terminal.
.TP
.B Mod+D
Open the launcher: the configured one, else
.BR fuzzel
(nothing where it isn't installed).
.TP
.B Mod
Tapped alone (pressed and released with no other key, click, scroll or gesture in between):
open the launcher too.
.TP
.BR Mod+Tab ", " Mod+J
Switch to the previous window; hold Mod and repeat for older ones.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+Tab
The same, the other way.
.TP
.BR Alt+Tab ", " Alt+Shift+Tab
The same as Mod+Tab and Mod+Shift+Tab, whatever Mod is.
.TP
.B Mod+W
The overview: the workspace's windows spread out, none over another, under a strip of every
workspace. Click a window, or pick it with the arrows and Enter; click a workspace to go there,
or drag a window onto it to send it there; Escape closes it.
.TP
.B Mod+M
Maximize; again, restore.
.TP
.B Mod+N
Minimize.
.TP
.B Mod+F
Fullscreen.
.TP
.BR Mod+Left ", " Mod+Right
Fill the left or right half of the screen (of a quarter: the other quarter beside it); toward
the side the window is already against, restore.
.TP
.B Mod+Up
Maximize; from a half, its top quarter; from a bottom quarter, the half; from a top quarter,
maximize.
.TP
.B Mod+Down
Restore a maximized window; from a half, its bottom quarter; from a top quarter, the half; from
a bottom quarter, restore.
.TP
.BR Mod+Shift+Left ", " Mod+Shift+Right ", " Mod+Shift+Up ", " Mod+Shift+Down
Move the window.
.TP
.BR Mod+Ctrl+Right ", " Mod+Ctrl+Down ", " Mod+Ctrl+Left ", " Mod+Ctrl+Up
Make the window wider, taller, narrower, shorter.
.TP
.B Mod+C
Center the window.
.TP
.B Mod+T
Keep the window above the others; again, stop.
.TP
.B Mod+P
Keep the window on all workspaces; again, stop.
.TP
.B Mod+H
Minimize the workspace's windows; again, bring them back.
.TP
.BR Mod+O ", " Mod+Shift+O
Go to the next monitor; send the window there.
.TP
.BR Mod+1 " .. " Mod+9 ", " Mod+Shift+1 " .. " Mod+Shift+9
Go to a workspace; send the window there.
.TP
.BR Mod+Page_Down ", " Mod+Page_Up
Next, previous workspace.
.TP
.BR Mod+Shift+Page_Down ", " Mod+Shift+Page_Up
Send the window to the next, previous workspace.
.TP
.BR Mod+WheelScrollDown ", " Mod+WheelScrollUp
Mod and the mouse wheel: next, previous workspace, a notch each (a quick turn is one:
.BR wheel-binding-cooldown ).
.TP
.BR Mod+Shift+WheelScrollDown ", " Mod+Shift+WheelScrollUp
Send the window to the next, previous workspace.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+S
Screenshot of a region to the clipboard (grim, slurp, wl-copy).
.TP
.B Mod+Ctrl+S
Screenshot of the monitor under the pointer to ~/Pictures (grim).
.TP
.BR XF86AudioRaiseVolume ", " XF86AudioLowerVolume
Volume up, down by 5% (wpctl).
.TP
.BR XF86AudioMute ", " XF86AudioMicMute
Mute or unmute the speakers, the microphone (wpctl).
.TP
.BR XF86MonBrightnessUp ", " XF86MonBrightnessDown
Screen brightness up, down by 5% (brightnessctl).
.TP
.BR XF86AudioPlay ", " XF86AudioPause ", " XF86AudioNext ", " XF86AudioPrev ", " XF86AudioStop
Play or pause, next, previous, stop, in the player that's playing (playerctl).
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+Q
Close the window.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+E
Quit Pila.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+slash
Show the cheatsheet of the shortcuts, in sections (Mod+?); on its first run Pila shows the few to
get going. Any key or click hides it.
.TP
.BR "Mod + left drag" ", " "Mod + right drag"
Move a window; resize it from the nearest corner.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Alt+F1 .. F12
Switch to another virtual terminal.
.PP
Touchpad swipes:
.B swipe\-3\-left
and
.B swipe\-3\-right
switch windows,
.B swipe\-3\-up
maximizes or restores,
.B swipe\-3\-down
restores,
.B swipe\-4\-left
and
.B swipe\-4\-right
go to the next and previous workspace,
.B swipe\-4\-up
and
.B swipe\-4\-down
open and close the overview.
.PP
The configuration's
.B [bindings]
and
.B [gestures]
change any of them but the mouse drags (which follow
.BR mod )
and the virtual terminal switch.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PILA_MOD
The Mod key:
.BR super ,
.B alt
or
.BR ctrl .
Overrides the configuration's
.BR mod .
.TP
.B PILA_TERMINAL
The terminal for Mod+Return.
Overrides
.BR terminal .
.TP
.B PILA_LAUNCHER
The command for Mod+D and for tapping Mod alone.
Overrides
.BR launcher .
.TP
.BR XCURSOR_THEME ", " XCURSOR_SIZE
The cursor theme and size when the configuration's
.B [cursor]
sets none (default: default, 24); nested, only for the cursor grown by shaking it. Set
there, they are also the ones of the programs Pila starts.
.TP
.BR XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT ", " XKB_DEFAULT_VARIANT ", " XKB_DEFAULT_OPTIONS
The keyboard layout when the configuration's
.B [keyboard]
leaves it empty.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
What the log shows (default: info; debug for details).
.PP
Programs Pila starts get its
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
and Xwayland's
.B DISPLAY
and
.B XAUTHORITY
(its cookie),
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP =pila,
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE =wayland
and
.B PILA_SOCKET
(for
.BR "pila msg" ),
and not other compositors' variables
.RB ( SWAYSOCK ", " HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE ", ...),"
so a shell drives Pila;
commands run by key bindings also get
.BR PILA_OUTPUT ,
the name of the monitor under the pointer.
.PP
On a TTY, Pila exports
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY ", " XAUTHORITY ", " XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ", " XDG_SESSION_TYPE
and
.B PILA_SOCKET
to the D\-Bus session bus (and to systemd \-\-user when that's the bus), for the
screen\-sharing portals and shells started as services.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.config/pila/config.toml " (or " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pila/config.toml )
The configuration. Every option is described in
.IR config.example.toml ,
installed with the documentation by Pila's packages; the first time Pila starts without the
file (and without
.BR \-\-config ),
it writes that example there.
Its active values are the defaults, and nowhere else: Pila has it built in and takes from it every
setting the configuration leaves out.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pila\-tools/
Pila's configuration for the tools the default configuration starts (waybar, fuzzel, mako,
swaylock and swaybg, which gets Pila's wallpaper), written at startup and named on their command
line where you have none of your own; nothing is written in
.IR ~/.config .
Copy one into
.I ~/.config
to change it.
.B tool\-configs = false
in the configuration turns it off.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/pila.log " (or " $XDG_STATE_HOME/pila/pila.log )
The log of a session started by
.BR pila\-session ;
.I pila.log.old
is the run before.
With systemd, also
.BR "journalctl \-t pila \-b" .
A crash is logged as "pila crashed", with where it happened.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/sessions.json " (or " $XDG_STATE_HOME/pila/sessions.json )
Where windows were, for apps that ask to get them back (xdg\-session\-management).
A nested Pila, or a second one started next to a session, keeps that in memory only.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pila\-WAYLAND_DISPLAY.sock
The socket of
.BR "pila msg" ,
in
.B PILA_SOCKET
for the programs Pila starts.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/pila.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/pila\-shutdown.target
With systemd,
.B pila\-session
runs Pila as this user service, bound to
.BR graphical\-session.target ,
which starts the session's user services and XDG autostart entries once Pila is ready and
stops them when it quits;
.B PILA_NO_SYSTEMD=1
makes it start Pila itself instead.
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/xdg\-desktop\-portal/pila\-portals.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/share/xdg\-desktop\-portal/portals/pila.portal
Which portals serve a Pila session: Pila's own for screen sharing, screenshots and apps' global
shortcuts, xdg\-desktop\-portal\-gtk for the rest.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B pila
exits with 2 for a wrong argument and not 0 if it can't start.
.B pila \-\-check\-config
exits with 0 if the configuration is valid and 1 if not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR Xwayland (1),
.BR xkeyboard\-config (7)
.PP
https://github.com/igalmarino/pila
